WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.290
- Our Patreon recommended

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.510
that I diagnose SpongeBob episodes.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.700
Let's just do Nickelodeon
and get a bunch in there.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.040
See if your favorite show's reviewed.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.910
- I'll cater to your every whim

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.160
until that leg of yours
is entirely healed.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.510
- I don't know what injury she has,

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.790
but that's a full leg cast above the knee.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.520
I don't know what injury she has.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.770
- Say, that's not a bad idea.

00:00:22.577 --> 00:00:24.119
Ow!

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:25.966
(vase crashing)

00:00:25.966 --> 00:00:28.000
Oh, oh!
- Angelica, what happened?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.680
- Munchausen syndrome in full effect.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.920
When a patient pretends to be ill

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.540
in order to gain
attention and/or sympathy.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.240
There's actually a Munchausen by proxy

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.210
where someone can actively
make their child ill

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.550
to get attention and sympathy
for the both of them.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.890
It's a really devastating illness

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.900
'cause it presents so many complications.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.480
The doctor has to even suspect

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.081
that it's potentially happening.

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:50.230
- What are you gonna do?

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.460
- I'm gonna take pictures of your insides.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.693
Say cheese!

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.620
- We don't give full body
X-rays for a leg pain.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.490
There's no point of that.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.330
It's just exposing you to more radiation.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.163
They're targeted.

00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:02.160
- It's kind of strange at first,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.243
but after a while you get to like it.

00:01:05.243 --> 00:01:07.920
- When the x-ray tech or
the radiology specialist

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.540
is in there with you, they
actually start the machine up,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.410
go behind a wall so they're protected

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.630
because otherwise they'd get exposed

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.920
to so much radiation over the dozens

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.700
and dozens of tests
they do in a given day.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.930
It's not healthy

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.000
- We'll be sick forever,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.100
unless I can extract some mitochondria

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.130
from the virus and make a vaccine.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.740
- Okay, medically he just spoke gibberish.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.130
Getting the mitochondria,
the energy center of the cell

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.390
not the powerhouse of the cell,

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.940
will not allow you to create a vaccine.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.240
If you wanna create a vaccine

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.030
you create a protein on the surface of it.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.180
Then you put it into the body

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.150
and allow the immune
system to recognize it.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.270
You know, the way that we
traditionally make vaccines.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.001
I don't know what he's talking about.

00:01:44.001 --> 00:01:46.668
(laser zapping)

00:01:47.554 --> 00:01:49.698
- Look, there's Carl's brain.

00:01:49.698 --> 00:01:52.190
- Where, I don't see it?

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:53.830
Is it behind that walnut on a stick?

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.479
- Your brain is a lot bigger than that.

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:57.750
♪ I've got people inside me ♪

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.170
Why whenever they do medical
procedures on cartoons

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.263
they strap people in like
it's a torture device?

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.890
All right, they just
traveled into the stomach.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.240
So right away, if this was to happen,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.290
the acid would melt their
little inflatable raft here,

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.630
and they would fall into the
acid, and lose their lives.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.790
Which is why, by the way,

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:19.300
drinking alkalized water
is complete horse poop.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.920
The reason I say it is because when you

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.390
drink the alkalized water,

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.700
that means it has like a pH
of eight instead of seven,

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.830
it falls into your stomach acid,
which has a pH of like two.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.890
What do you think happens
to the pH of eight

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.010
when it mixes with a huge
container of pH of two?

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.923
No longer alkaline.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.620
- Well, where to next, Douglas?

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.570
The mountains, the arctic?

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.780
- I feel like these are the
current devices we have now.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.530
You hop on a Peloton and it's like

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.740
where do you wanna ride,
through what trail?

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.440
And to me, I understand a lot of people

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:51.700
don't have time to go outside,

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.860
or the weather's not cooperative,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.990
but like folks, spend some time outdoors.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.271
It's cool too.

00:02:56.271 --> 00:02:57.104
(Doug grunting)

00:02:57.104 --> 00:03:00.330
- No, no, you need to
start out slow, Doug.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:04.403
See, the important thing
is not to strain yourself.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:06.810
- Amazing advice!

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.450
No joke, in a cartoon.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.130
Right now, I had to just
get back into working out.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.990
I used to bench like 255,
285 during my workouts.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.940
The other day I was
doing 95, and it's okay.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.490
It bruises the ego, but it
strengthens the muscles.

00:03:20.352 --> 00:03:21.580
(bubbles popping)

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:22.870
- Nothing like a sea-nut butter

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.237
and jellyfish jelly sandwich.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:28.732
- Ew, sea-nut butter, that sounds gross.

00:03:28.732 --> 00:03:30.082
- To help you get to sleep.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.400
- And ideally you don't wanna
eat too close to bedtime

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.510
'cause then your stomach produces

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.130
all those gastric juices, highly acidic.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.140
And then when you lie down,

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:39.430
and you're parallel to the floor,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.960
and the gastric juices starts
coming up, you get heartburn.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.860
(Gary snoring)

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.010
Smelly fridge syndrome?

00:03:48.408 --> 00:03:50.910
(SpongeBob groaning)

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:51.920
- The fridge!

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.630
- No lie, that's how I sleep.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.530
I love it freezing.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.250
I like to sleep with multiple covers,

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:58.790
with a hoodie on sometimes.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.480
I actually just got one of
these cooling mattresses.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.100
Maybe it's 'cause I grew up in Siberia.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.600
And when I was in my mom's womb

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.080
I was like freezing all the time.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.220
So I wanna go back to that.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.058
That's why I always sleep
in the fetal position.

00:04:09.058 --> 00:04:10.250
(Gary meows)

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.090
- Don't be silly Gary, I don't get colds.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.183
I get the suds.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.020
No Gary, if I had the suds

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.238
I'd have bubbles coming out of me.

00:04:20.238 --> 00:04:22.470
(SpongeBob sneezes)

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.460
- Oh no, he has the suds.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:25.960
Grandma was right to a degree.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.780
If you go outside and your hair
is wet, or you get a chill,

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.660
it doesn't mean that
you're gonna get sick.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.470
It can just make you a
little bit more susceptible

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.434
than you were before.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:37.400
- Hey, SpongeBob.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.500
- I'm sick, Patrick

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.550
- I'm gonna go ahead and guess
that he has a adenovirus.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.940
'Cause you know, he has the
conjunctiva that's inflamed,

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.530
he has the upper respiratory symptoms.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:45.670
I don't know if he has diarrhea.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.190
I mean, he probably doesn't have diarrhea

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.960
'cause he's a cartoon sponge after all.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.806
But if a cartoon sponge
was to have diarrhea,

00:04:50.806 --> 00:04:52.227
and inflamed conjunctiva,

00:04:52.227 --> 00:04:54.761
and upper respiratory
symptoms, adenovirus.

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:59.330
- I know a guy who knows a guy
who went to the doctor once

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.940
and the doctor's office is
a horrible, horrible place!

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:03.870
- It is not!

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.203
We have fun there!

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.273
- Well first, they make you
sit in the waiting room.

00:05:10.273 --> 00:05:12.460
- Is that the horrible part, Patrick?

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.800
- No, it gets worse!

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.053
They make you read old magazines!

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.460
- I don't know why that is.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.530
Can we update our magazine collection

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.640
in the medical offices for God's sake?

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:27.520
Who wants to read about
People magazine from 2006?

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.510
I mean, we know Britney
was killing it back then,

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:30.870
but we wanna know what's up now.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.070
Is that the year she was killing it?

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:36.210
- Then the doctor pulls
out his stethoscope!

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.390
It's a device so sinister, so icy cold

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.007
when it touches your
bare flesh, it (hisses).

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:46.490
- Honestly, the cold stethoscope is one of

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.660
the meanest things a
provider can do for you.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.620
Every time before I make
contact with a patient's chest

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.001
and/or back to listen to
lungs, I do one of these.

00:05:55.001 --> 00:05:56.230
(teapot whistling)

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.270
And I get of the warmth of
my hand onto the stethoscope.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.280
It's the least you can
do for your patients!

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.070
- You gotta help me get better, Patrick.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:04.903
Please.

00:06:04.903 --> 00:06:07.200
- I'm so excited to see what
Patrick's about to recommend.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.830
If he says Manuka honey,

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.686
I might give a starfish a high-five.

00:06:10.686 --> 00:06:13.300
(SpongeBob sneezes)

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.570
I have been a doctor for
like seven, eight years.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:19.350
Medical school, four years.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.980
I do not know what that
thing is on Patrick's head

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.840
that doctors used to wear back in the day.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.170
It doesn't make sense.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.420
It reflects light.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.940
It has a hole in the middle.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.240
I have no idea what it is, what it does.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.440
I've even worn it as part
of a Halloween costume.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.590
- Oh my God, I actually
look like a doctor.

00:06:38.701 --> 00:06:42.610
- It appears as though we'll
have to plug up these holes.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.223
This oughta do the trick.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:49.263
Voila!

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.548
Feel better?

00:06:51.548 --> 00:06:52.381
- I don't know.

00:06:52.381 --> 00:06:53.900
- I'm gonna do a hyper-analysis here.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.850
See what's happening
is, instead of treating

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:58.620
SpongeBob's core illness, he's
treating the symptoms only.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:06:59.900
If this is a viral illness,

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.800
that's a perfectly acceptable
way of going about it.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.390
To allow the immune system time to heal,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.190
to rest, to improve itself.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.550
But if it's a bacterial infection

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:08.090
we're missing it, and
we're treating symptoms,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.310
and not treating the underlying cause.

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:12.910
Could be a big cause for
concern there, Patrick.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.090
My lawyers will be in touch.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.850
Or as my legal legal eagle friend says it,

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.955
I'll see you in court!
- I'll see you in court.

00:07:18.955 --> 00:07:21.973
(Patrick grunts)

00:07:21.973 --> 00:07:23.582
- Feeling better yet?

00:07:23.582 --> 00:07:24.730
- Not really.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:26.352
- Why'd he pull out his tooth?

00:07:26.352 --> 00:07:27.685
- How about now?

00:07:30.038 --> 00:07:32.490
- (grunting) I don't think so.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.670
- Actually, you may think this is silly,

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:36.420
this is something we sort
of do in the hospitals.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.200
It's called chest physical therapy.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:39.910
If a patient has a lot of mucus,

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:42.290
we actually have specialized
beds that perform

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.760
this like thumping feature
that helps clear out the mucus.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.600
In patients who have cystic fibrosis.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.270
we actually need this type
of chest physical therapy

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.410
to be done on a regular
basis in order to help them

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:55.170
expel that mucus and prevent
blockages in the airways.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:56.510
Kind of crazy that SpongeBob has

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.090
some level of medical accuracy, who knew?

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:01.232
- So it seems you have the suds.

00:08:01.232 --> 00:08:03.137
Are you ready for your treatment?

00:08:03.137 --> 00:08:05.159
- The doctor's wearing the thing again,

00:08:05.159 --> 00:08:06.723
what is that thing, what is it doing?

00:08:07.696 --> 00:08:09.090
And why is there a fish anatomy

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.690
in the background when
there's a sponge patient?

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:12.923
Different species.

00:08:14.408 --> 00:08:16.570
- Well it looks like your gluteus maximus

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.060
has made a full recovery.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:19.270
- Ooh, his gluteus maximus.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:21.060
They're using proper anatomical terms.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.220
I guess he tore a muscle in his buttock.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.490
- Your butt's all better.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:26.080
It's really quite amazing.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.770
- I love that they did an x-ray.

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:31.240
First of all, you can't
x-ray gluteus maximus.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:32.360
You can't x-ray muscles.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.330
You can x-ray bones,
joints, but not muscles.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.330
- It took 20 hours to
put it all back together.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.890
We actually ran out of staples
and had to use a glue stick.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.090
- There is a fact here.

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:45.100
In closing skin, there
are both of those options.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.870
For example, if you get
a cut on your skull,

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:48.680
you can actually close it with staples.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:49.610
It works quite well.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:51.330
If you get a small cut on your hand

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.460
we have skin glue that
can keep it together.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:54.293
Isn't that cool?

00:08:54.293 --> 00:08:55.143
SpongeBob, what's up?

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.510
- Ah doctor, thank goodness you're here.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.590
- They're wearing the
thing on the heads again!

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.564
- And what seems to be the problem today?

00:09:02.564 --> 00:09:04.080
(patient coughing)

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.570
- My throat hurts!

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.110
- Why is this patient admitted inpatient

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:09.930
into a hospital for a throat ache?

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.005
With again, a chart on the bedside

00:09:12.005 --> 00:09:14.070
that looks like a stock market chart

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:15.390
more than it does an EKG?

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:16.790
What even is that?

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:19.073
- I'll need one medical sea chicken.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.030
- What's a medical sea chicken?

00:09:26.761 --> 00:09:29.678
(patient giggling)

00:09:30.607 --> 00:09:33.060
(patient coughs)

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.904
- Hey, my keys!

00:09:34.904 --> 00:09:36.970
And my throat feels better.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:40.030
- He's not a doctor of humans.

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:43.510
He's a vet because they're in a fish tank,

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:45.750
or at the bottom of the ocean.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.363
And there's a catfish that ate some keys.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:55.630
And didn't have strep
throat, but had key throat!

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:57.130
- [Man] You sold that one.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.617
Subscribe!

00:09:58.617 --> 00:10:00.784
- Key throat.
- Key throat.

00:10:02.017 --> 00:10:03.630
- Oh no, a little laryngitis.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:06.340
- Oh those tonsils are
really enlarged, Gerald.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:07.540
- Oh, tonsillitis.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:08.880
- I think we should take them out.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.700
- Now we're trying to move away
from doing tonsillectomies.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.560
We're weighing the risks
versus benefits here.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.360
And we have a set of
guidelines that we follow.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:20.150
If you have X number of strep
throats, or tonsillitis,

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.887
or pharyngitis, that we
get you set up with an ENT

00:10:22.887 --> 00:10:23.990
who then makes the decision

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.020
whether or not they need to come out.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.319
Now we're trying to do our
best to manage it medically

00:10:27.319 --> 00:10:30.090
and not have to resort
to a surgical operation

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:30.923
if we don't need to.

00:10:30.923 --> 00:10:32.140
That doesn't mean you won't get surgery.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:34.720
I'm just saying, there's
options on the table.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:35.553
Ow.

00:10:35.553 --> 00:10:36.860
- What am I gonna do, Arnold?

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:38.980
- Maybe if you gargle or something.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:40.970
- Gargling is actually not a bad option.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:42.490
Also, honey.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.100
You know I love my honey.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:47.920
- Are you having problems
with your you know what?

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.250
- No, my fagiggly gland is fine.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.350
- His what gland?

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.900
- No, it's not.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:54.770
Cosmo's fagiggly gland is--

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:56.100
- Fagiggly gland.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:58.080
I wonder what the fagiggly gland is.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.220
- The special, magical
organ in a fairy's body

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:03.003
that allows us to change shape.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.350
- What?
- Is Cosmo sick?

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.081
- Let me put this to you in
terms Cosmo could understand.

00:11:10.081 --> 00:11:11.170
Yes!

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.080
- Why does the doctor keep
putting the glasses on,

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:13.913
taking them off?

00:11:13.913 --> 00:11:15.480
And why is the stethoscope floating?

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.470
I need ex--
And he's wearing that thing!

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:18.992
What is that thing?

00:11:18.992 --> 00:11:20.490
(kids groaning)

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:23.112
Oh no, walking dead again.

00:11:23.112 --> 00:11:25.862
(dramatic music)

00:11:27.841 --> 00:11:29.191
Why are they all jaundiced?

00:11:30.648 --> 00:11:31.772
(kid yells)

00:11:31.772 --> 00:11:33.550
- What the?
- He looks okay.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:37.770
- 986 degrees.

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.900
- 98.6 degrees, normal body temperature.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.710
- this morning our house became infested

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:45.370
with an acute febrile contagious virus,

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:48.680
or as it's known on the street, the flu.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.190
- Ooh, the flu.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.530
We haven't been seeing
much of that lately.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.490
- With 13 Louds packed
into 1200 square feet,

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:56.770
when someone gets sick it
spreads like the plague.

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:57.770
- That's true, actually.

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.360
In like college dorm
rooms, military barracks,

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.150
anytime you have people
living in close proximity

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:03.690
it's very easy for one person to get sick,

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:05.100
and then to get everybody else sick.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:05.933
- In case we're attacked,

00:12:05.933 --> 00:12:08.603
this concoction will
slow down the infected.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.130
- What is it, boogers?

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.810
- Tastes like chicken soup.
- Oh.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:14.710
- That's because it is chicken soup.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.520
- I'd be just shooting
that in my mouth all day.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.094
I love chicken soup.

00:12:18.094 --> 00:12:20.030
(Sokka yelling)

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:21.185
- They won't let go!

00:12:21.185 --> 00:12:22.018
Help!

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.320
- Stop making so much noise.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:26.910
It's just a purple pentapus.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.800
- Oh, it's a purple pentapus.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.250
Never heard of a purple pentapus.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:31.800
I don't know why this popped into my head.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.020
Have you ever heard of
molluscum contagiosum?

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.570
It sounds really serious,
but it's not really

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.530
that scary of a virus that causes

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.990
these little skin pearly
papules to pop up.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:42.240
It happens more often in kids.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:43.520
People who share towels.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.470
Sometimes they're sexually transferred.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.510
I like when they just rub
'em like that, (squeaking)

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:53.910
and they just pop right off.

00:12:54.823 --> 00:12:57.399
(raucous music)

00:12:57.399 --> 00:12:58.232
Organ school.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:01.266
- Good morning, class.

00:13:01.266 --> 00:13:03.411
- [Students] Good morning, Miss Pancreas.

00:13:03.411 --> 00:13:05.243
- Miss Pancreas!

00:13:05.243 --> 00:13:06.624
- Miss Pancreas.

00:13:06.624 --> 00:13:07.457
- Yes, Bladder?

00:13:07.457 --> 00:13:08.910
- That doesn't look like a bladder,

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:10.150
that looks like a lemon.

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:11.500
- Can I go to the bathroom?

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:13.360
- And ironic that the bladder asked to go.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:16.100
- [Miss Pancreas] You'll have
to wait 'til after roll call.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:17.570
- Oh my god, this is so embarrassing.

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:19.460
I remember in like second or third grade

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:21.920
I asked the teacher to go to
the bathroom and she said no.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.853
And I peed myself in class.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.140
Someone said like, oh, what's
that liquid next to your desk?

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:28.790
I'm like, I spilled a
water bottle. (laughing)

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:30.610
Thank God my pants dried quickly.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:31.610
So embarrassing!

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:33.560
I can't believe I'm talking about it.

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:35.800
Kids sometimes ask me
weird medical questions.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:37.080
Click here to check that out.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.115
And as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:13:39.115 --> 00:13:41.698
(upbeat music)

